BPF 4.19 compatibility follow-up
================================

Host checks (bounds, allocation and kernel services use stubs):

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_adjust_room_eth.py
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_global_memory_args.py
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_helper_memory_modifiers.py

The Ethernet check executes bpf_skb_net_grow() and checks ETH_P_TEB,
inner header offsets, short Ethernet headers and unchanged legacy behavior.
It does not validate real skb movement, GSO or actual packet transmission.
The global argument check executes BTF matching and argument preparation;
the memory check executes verifier helper/global memory dispatch.

On-device checks still required:

* Load global functions taking scalar/struct pointers; check NULL branches,
  stack/map bounds, uninitialized input rejection and readonly input rejection.
* Attach tracing programs to migrate_disable/migrate_enable, and to
  preempt_count_add/sub when built as functions: loading must fail. Normal
  kernel targets and BPF-program targets must retain their existing behavior.
* Exercise TC adjust_room Ethernet encapsulation with IPv4/IPv6 traffic and
  GSO, then inspect the resulting packets and segmentation behavior.
* With NET=n and BPF_SYSCALL=y, create TASK_STORAGE maps and exercise task
  exit and map deletion. Socket storage remains conditional on networking.

Temporary out-of-tree configs are used for build validation. No defconfig
changes are part of this patch. On this 4.19 tree BPF_JIT is inside the NET
Kconfig menu, so NET=n exercises the interpreter/non-JIT path as well.
The tracing denylist omits rcu_read_unlock_strict, absent from this tree,
and includes preempt_count_add/sub only under their definition conditions.
The denylist applies to kernel BTF IDs, not unrelated module/BPF IDs.
